<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Operates – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Town Operates</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose essays on general life experiences. Interactive: Participate in text-based discussions like messages, email discussions, chats, or online forums.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose essays on civic engagement, using examples from the Town Council scenario (e.g., "Citizens must vote to have a say in community decisions."). Can participate in online discussions about civic responsibilities, posting comments about local issues (e.g., "I believe citizens should attend town meetings to share their views."). Can respond to classmates' posts in a discussion forum, adding perspectives on community involvement (e.g., "In my opinion, volunteering improves our town.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of a local town meeting or a public service announcement about civic duties. Students are asked to write 2-3 sentences in their notebooks explaining what they think are the most important 'responsibilities' of citizens in their 'community' and why. They must use at least one vocabulary word from previous lessons (e.g., 'participate', 'vote', 'decisions').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a sentence related to civic engagement on the board, using one of the target grammatical structures. For instance, "New 'projects' for 'sustainability' 'are being discussed' by the 'town council'." or "The mayor 'reported' that 'public services' 'would be improved' soon." The teacher emphasizes the use of 'passive voice' and 'reported speech'. The teacher also models the pronunciation of 'public' /ɪ/ and 'serve' /e/ + /r/ while reading the sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled sentence structures.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is discussing the projects in the first sentence? Is it specified?" (Checks understanding of passive voice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are the public services improved yet, or will they be in the future, according to the mayor?" (Checks understanding of reported speech).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher provides a short excerpt from an online discussion forum post from the local Town Council's website, where citizens are 'sharing viewpoints' on how best to 'contribute' to 'community involvement'. The text will include examples of 'passive voice', 'reported speech', and 'modals' like 'should' and 'must', along with key vocabulary related to 'civic duties' and 'municipal government'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short online forum post/comment (approx. 150 words), informal yet informative register, digital format (projected on screen or printed handout).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding different perspectives and viewpoints expressed in online discussions related to civic engagement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the post individually.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of discussion in the forum post?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases from the post to their rephrased meanings (e.g., "Decisions are made based on community feedback" matches "The community's opinions guide the council's choices").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Target grammar (Passive voice, Reported speech, Modals: 'can', 'should', 'must', 'may', 'might') and vocabulary (nouns: 'town council', 'community', 'voice', 'decisions', 'public services', 'elections', 'responsibilities', 'citizens', 'participation', 'voting'; verbs: 'operate', 'provide', 'participate', 'vote', 'report', 'attend', 'contribute', 'decide', 'express', 'inform', 'engage'; adjectives: 'civic', 'responsible', 'sustainable', 'essential', 'informed').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences related to 'Our Town Council' using the correct form of the passive voice, reported speech, or an appropriate modal verb, along with provided vocabulary words. Example: "Many important 'decisions' _______ (make) by the 'town council' every month." (Answer: are made). "The councilor _______ (say) that citizens _______ (should/must) 'participate' more in 'local' 'projects'." (Answer: said, should/must).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students rewrite active sentences into passive, direct speech into reported speech, or simple statements into opinions using modals. Example: "The citizens elect the authorities." -> "The 'authorities' 'are elected' by the 'citizens'." "Our town needs more green spaces." -> "Citizens 'should' 'express' their 'voice' about green spaces."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Sentence Review):</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed and transformed sentences. They 'show respect in public dialogue' by giving constructive feedback on grammar, vocabulary use, and clarity. They ensure the sentences are relevant to 'civic' 'responsibilities' and 'community involvement'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: Online Civic Engagement Forum Post.</b> Students will compose an argumentative paragraph and respond to classmates' posts in a simulated online forum (e.g., using a collaborative online document or a shared digital board).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual writing of an argumentative paragraph (approx. 6-8 sentences) followed by interactive written responses to two classmates' posts (3-4 sentences each).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Initial Post:</b> Students choose one civic responsibility from a list (e.g., 'voting' in 'elections', 'attending town meetings', 'volunteering' for 'community projects'). They write a paragraph arguing why this specific responsibility is 'essential' for 'How Our Town Operates' effectively, using examples from the 'Town Council scenario'. They must include at least one sentence in the 'passive voice', one using 'reported speech', and two 'modal verbs' ('should', 'must', 'can', 'may', 'might') to 'share viewpoints'. They are encouraged to use 'formal language in civic discussions'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Responses:</b> Once paragraphs are posted, students read two classmates' contributions. For each, they write a response post (3-4 sentences) where they 'ask for opinions on civic responsibilities' or 'share viewpoints on community involvement', agreeing or disagreeing respectfully with their classmate's point and adding their own perspective. They should aim to 'show respect in public dialogue'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment based on a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their initial forum post and their two responses to classmates. Peers use a checklist to evaluate one another's written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric/Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the main argument in the initial post.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target grammar (passive voice, reported speech, modals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary related to 'civic engagement'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relevance and respectful tone in responses to classmates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fulfillment of task requirements (length, number of specific grammar points).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflective prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing your forum post or responding to your classmates?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary or grammar structure did you feel most confident using in your writing today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did expressing your opinion in writing about civic responsibilities make you feel about your role in the community?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned today about structuring an argument or giving feedback respectfully in writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The writing lesson effectively integrated grammar, vocabulary, and pragmatic functions. Students demonstrated a good understanding of 'passive voice' and 'modals' in their initial posts, but some struggled with 'reported speech' accuracy. The online forum activity fostered good interactive writing, with most students applying formal language. Adjustments needed for next lesson include providing more explicit sentence frames for reported speech to reinforce accuracy. Scaffolds for students who struggled with paragraph organization could include a simple essay outline for the next writing task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their understanding of 'How Our Town Operates' by summarizing and presenting their civic engagement ideas from today's writing activity. They will integrate the concepts of 'community involvement' and 'civic duties' into a collaborative 21st Century Skills Project, potentially creating a public awareness campaign based on their written arguments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>